# Framework 13 AMD: rescue the external monitor a USB-C dock forgot to
# announce. See /usr/libexec/margine/dock-dp-rescue for the evidence and
# for why the workaround is gated to one machine.
#
# The trigger is the USB-C partner appearing, which is the moment a dock
# is plugged in. The helper then waits, does nothing if DisplayPort shows
# up on its own, and only re-initialises the controller if it does not.
#
# systemd-run, not a direct RUN+=: udev kills anything still running
# after a short timeout, and this helper deliberately waits several
# seconds before deciding. Running it inline would guarantee it is killed
# before it can do anything useful.
#
# The fixed unit name is deliberate: the rebind re-creates port0-partner,
# which fires this rule again while the first run is still sleeping, and
# systemd refusing a second unit of the same name is exactly the
# de-duplication wanted. --collect keeps that from being a trap, and the
# trap is not hypothetical: on 2026-08-25 the helper had a bug that made
# it exit 1 after a SUCCESSFUL rescue, the failed unit stayed loaded, and
# the next four plug-ins fired this rule and were all refused a unit of
# that name. Nothing was logged, because nothing ran. With --collect a
# failed unit is unloaded as soon as it stops, so a bad run costs one
# rescue, not every rescue until reboot.
#
# Measured on the reference host 2026-08-25: dock plugged in with the
# screen dark, rule fired, rescue logged, screen up seven seconds later.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="typec", KERNEL=="port[0-9]-partner", \
  RUN+="/usr/bin/systemd-run --no-block --collect --unit=margine-dock-dp-rescue --description='Rescue a dock display the USB-C negotiation missed' /usr/libexec/margine/dock-dp-rescue"
